Fallon Looney: That should be easy.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Whitney Hodges: Hey, Lauren!

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Lauren Christensen: Hi there!

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Fallon Looney: Hi!

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Lauren Christensen: Hi.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Whitney Hodges: How are you? Good to see you.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Lauren Christensen: You too!

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Whitney Hodges: Where are you right now? It looks like you have fun inventory behind you.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, um, this is our showroom, so this is, like, all of our rental decor, um…

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Whitney Hodges: So pretty.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Lauren Christensen: And we've got, like, a little lounge in here and stuff for, um, the client. So it's not connected to the actual venue, it's, like, right across. We have a little driveway and a parking lot, and there'.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Whitney Hodges: Nice. Love it. Okay, awesome. Well, it's good to see you. Thanks for meeting with us again. Um, you did a really good job of filling out the onboarding questionnaire, so thank you for that. That context is, like, super helpful for us going into this call, and it just means that we can, like.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, of course.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.000
Whitney Hodges: dive even deeper, sort of thing. So, Fallon has kind of, like, our standard, like.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Whitney Hodges: messaging and positioning interview questions that we're gonna just kind of go through with you today. Um, and sometimes we'll jump around a little bit, like, sometimes if we feel like something, especially in your current marketing or your, you know, onboarding questionnaire that you filled out, if we feel like we have a really good handle on that already, we may not dig into that area or dig into it.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Lauren Christensen: Okay.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, as much. So just kind of be aware of that. Um, before we do dive into that, I did want to circle back because I think the one thing as far as like tech connections that we didn't get covered on the first call was that Google analytics piece of it. So just wanted to see if you had had a chance to reach out to.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Lauren Christensen: Okay.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.000
Whitney Hodges: Elizabeth, and if you had heard anything in that regard.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, so last week, I think on Friday, or maybe it was even over the weekend, um…

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Lauren Christensen: I… Amy actually basically, I think, started a new Google Analytics, and then yesterday, she basically sent me a draft email to send to Elizabeth, saying, hey, this is what I need from you. I think she has to invite… she invited, essentially, myself.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Whitney Hodges: Oh, okay.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.000
Whitney Hodges: Unencryption.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:11.000
Lauren Christensen: and Elizabeth to that, and is asking Elizabeth to transfer it over or something. Um, so I responded to her because I had one other question about, I think, like, the DNS records with the hosting. So I was trying to wait to see if I could, you know, get all the info from Elizabeth in one email. So, um, I haven't heard back from Amy as of this morning, but it's…

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, got it.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm. That's true.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:25.000
Lauren Christensen: essentially, it's in process, which hopefully should… I should be able to get that email out today. And then I have my onboarding call with Amy, um, at 1 o'clock today. So even if I don't hear from her via email, we're gonna talk on the phone, so I should have it all squared away for you guys by the end of the day.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:37.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, perfect, yeah, just keep us posted, like, once that new one is kind of in place and it has in it what it needs, um, we can give you or give Amy the instructions on adding us as an admin there.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Lauren Christensen: Perfect, yeah, because I'm, like, logged into it, and now I have access, there's just no information there, so I guess she's asking.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Whitney Hodges: Sure.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Whitney Hodges: Makes sense. Okay, perfect. Thanks for the update.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:52.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay. Well, Fallon, do you want to kind of take it from here and dig in?

00:05:52.000 --> 00:06:14.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah, I think that would be great. As Whitney mentioned, we might skip around on some of these questions, but I'm going to go ahead and share my screen just so that you can see the questions as we go through. I'm probably not going to be typing in the text box verbatim here because we'll have a recap summary. I want to make sure I'm really listening and taking it all in.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Fallon Looney: Um, but I guess really quickly, we can kind of start off with your brand story. Um, how did your business start? I know we also spoke about this a little bit on our last call, but is there anything important that you think from the lens of.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.000
Lauren Christensen: Okay.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:38.000
Fallon Looney: our ads messaging that would be worth noting in terms of the history of the business.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, I mean, I'm open to what you guys… how much you think is important versus not, because it's kind of a long story, but, you know, I have… obviously, my background in the bridal industry started almost two decades ago, um, so more in that bridal design with accessories.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:09.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, at the same time, you know, so it's myself and some partners that own the business, um, we all have, you know, over two decades of hospitality experience, more specifically in, um, managing restaurants and bars. Um, so we did start… originally actually purchase our building, um.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, to move our restaurant into it. So our restaurant's located right around the corner. Um…

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:32.000
Lauren Christensen: but in terms of, like, from a marketing and messaging perspective, I mean, typically when I'm telling that story, it's just to kind of, like, build expertise and show that we have experience, you know, doing hospitality now for a while. Um, so, you know, sometimes we talk about, you know, just the experience-backed team, and it's a group of hospitality professionals that have been doing this for a long time.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:49.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, you know, we have, obviously, the catering, and we do it, we've done events off-site all around the county for, you know, over 12 years now. Um, but we bring that expertise, and those are more luxury events, like high production, so we take that really experienced, like, um, you know, professional skill level, and bring it to our.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:08:00.000
Lauren Christensen: more attainable, you know, kind of… I won't say budget-friendly, but, you know, some… we take all that stuff that typically would be a luxury experience and kind of distill it down for something that's a little bit more approachable for us, if that makes sense.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:11.000
Fallon Looney: Yep, I think that's great. Is there anything specific that inspired you in terms of entering the wedding industry?

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:29.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, for myself specifically, since I am more of a creative at heart, just the opportunity for, like, the creative expression through weddings is really what kind of drove me there. Um, you know, started with my own personal wedding and kind of, um, designing some things for that, really falling in love with that, you know, and seeing how I could take.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:45.000
Lauren Christensen: the creative side of what I did and actually turn that into a career, um, in a way that is kind of ever evolving and changing, you know, I love that every wedding is different, every event looks different, so typically, you know, I was much more on the fashion side, but now I'm much more on the kind of interior design and decor side of things, but it all kind of.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:51.000
Lauren Christensen: relays together, so I think that's how… what inspired me, and what I like about it still.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:09:02.000
Fallon Looney: I love that. We can dive into, I think, mission and vision a bit more deeply. What would you say is the core mission of.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:03.000
Fallon Looney: of your business.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:20.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, I would say to produce weddings that look beautiful for our clients, but more importantly than that, I mean, we want them to look nice, but we want that experience for them to be as stress-free as possible. The all-inclusive package, really, the goal of that is to kind of.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:38.000
Lauren Christensen: take the burden of the planning and the micromanaging off of our clients' shoulders, allow the people that, you know, our team who does it every weekend to focus on those parts of it so that they have the energy and the desire to focus on the fun things. Because what, you know, we've always found was before we had these all-inclusive packages.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:55.000
Lauren Christensen: clients are so excited to book, and then we'd talk with them a month later to kind of follow up and see where they're at, and everyone's so overwhelmed with making all these decisions. Um, you know, and our clients don't have the budget to pay a full-service wedding planner, um, but their priority also, you know, even if they had the money, they probably wouldn't use it to spend on the.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:14.000
Lauren Christensen: a reflection of their experience and their values, which is not spending probably $250,000 on a wedding. They really just want it to be a great experience for their guests, so I think our overall mission is just to create an experience for them that actually allows them to enjoy the wedding planning process, and typically what that is is taking those small details off their plate, so.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:31.000
Lauren Christensen: They can focus on whatever's important to them. So that's, if that's, you know, having a lot of fun with the food and drink and getting to, you know, focus on those things, that's great. If it's, they do like decor and design and they really want to put some energy into that, that's awesome. But regardless of what it's important to them, they at least have the freedom to do that or the energy because they.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:46.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah. I love that. That's amazing. I guess you I feel like you really answered this question in in that as far as, like, your role in creating that unforgettable wedding experience.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:58.000
Fallon Looney: Is there anything that you would say outside of the planning process that you would add to your role in creating that unforgettable experience, say, on the day of?

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:58.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:15.000
Lauren Christensen: Well, yeah, I mean, I think our role is using that experience that we have from working these, you know, working for so long in the industry in typically much more complicated, like logistically complicated places, distilling all that experience down to create really seamless events on the day. So, you know, that starts with.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:33.000
Lauren Christensen: back-end communication with the client, understanding what their wants and needs are, but, you know, all of that planning and communication and management of vendors and stuff on the day, so that for the, you know, the guests look back and all they remember is the great experience. They don't remember anything going wrong, or, you know, even if something goes wrong, they don't know about it. You know, it's just a seamless experience on the day.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:49.000
Fallon Looney: Yep. Amazing. Are there any core values that you feel drive the business? And then how do those values influence the interactions and overall experience with the couples and their families in the process?

00:11:49.000 --> 00:12:12.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, I mean, I think one of our core values is just a service mindset, right? So we are here to serve our clients in whatever capacity that looks like. So that's one. And that also, you know, extends beyond our clients to our vendors and people that we work with. You know, we are here, our job is to serve people and give them what they need.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:32.000
Lauren Christensen: that's the same with our vendors, you know, we want them to feel like this is a place they want to come to, that we are on a team together, that we are here to make their work experience as good as well. Beyond that, in terms of core values, I mean, from a company standpoint, I want this to be a really great place for our staff to work, so I think a lot of that has to do with.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:47.000
Lauren Christensen: you know, we're a small team, um, so we're really transparent, and we're open with each other, um, we're all kind of working together to make things happen. We're not… nothing isn't our job, right? We're… we're a small team, so we have our hands in a lot of different, um, pots, but making sure that everyone feels appreciated, and…

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:58.000
Lauren Christensen: are informed, but are also, you know, comfortable to give feedback and provide input to the team overall. Just, you know, strong company culture is important to us as well.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:17.000
Fallon Looney: That's amazing. Um, I feel like I have a pretty solid understanding, just based on everything from your form and your site, but as far as ideal client profile, could you quickly summarize your ideal bride slash couple, and any common characteristics of.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:33.000
Fallon Looney: your most satisfied clients, mainly because this is going to really help us, especially with Meta, um, influence, like, the creative, and also, like, those, you know, headlines and captions specifically, so that we're calling out.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:49.000
Lauren Christensen: In terms of, like, a more general ideal client, um, I can give you that, and then give you, like, some actual specifics, too. Um, so generally, you know, mid to late 20s to kind of mid 30s, um.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:14:14.000
Lauren Christensen: Demographically, they mostly live either south or north of us. We're basically in the middle of California. So we're like three hours from LA, three hours from the Bay Area. So most of our clients are coming from metropolitan areas, but specifically choosing the Central Coast where we live because it's not the city, you know, it's a little bit more spacious. We're 15 minutes from the beach, we're five minutes from, you know, a plethora of wineries. So we're a destination location.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:32.000
Lauren Christensen: and they're specifically coming out of the city to get out of that city experience, have a little bit more space here, and they're drawn to Central Coast for that reason. I would say, you know, from what they do career-wise, most of them are, you know, college-educated, a lot of them working in tech in the Bay or LA.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:44.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, but either way, whatever they're doing, it's typically, um, you have big jobs, you know, corporate jobs where they're really busy working, you know, 40-plus hours a week. So, um, while they want their wedding to be, like I said.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:15:05.000
Lauren Christensen: memorable and excited, they don't really have the time or the desire to like spend a year and a half planning their wedding. They want to kind of show up to a place that looks beautiful. The experience is great, but they don't have to worry about it and they're willing to kind of pay for that up to a point, right? But they're not willing to spend six figures on their wedding either. You know, they'd rather take that money and purchase a house with it.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:12.000
Lauren Christensen: They want something that's not like an all-consuming wedding experience, wedding planning experience for them.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.000
Lauren Christensen: I would say…

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:34.000
Lauren Christensen: and then in terms of, like, specifics, so I would say, just for context, like, probably 80 to 85% of our clients, our wedding clients, are not from the area. Uh, but most of the time, they have some sort of connection to San Luis Obispo, and the biggest, kind of, client pool, which really does seem to be our ideal client, is, um, we have Cal Poly, our university here, it's a state university.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:49.000
Lauren Christensen: At least 60% of our out-of-town clients went to Cal Poly, at least one of them. Most of the time, it's like they went to Cal Poly, they met here in San Luis Obispo, they fell in love, and then they've moved on, you know, when they've graduated. So they want to come back to this spot that it all.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:16:09.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, and so they're, you know, they have the nostalgic and, you know, the tide of the San Luis Obispo area. They fit our bill demographically in terms of, like, they have these really great jobs. Cal Poly's a very well-respected school, so, um, especially engineering, um, is huge there. Architecture and then construction management, so we… they're… have gone on to have these really great jobs.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:19.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, they have, you know, pretty high income level, um, and so for them, the idea to get buried in slow, where it all started, but they don't have to worry about doing all the things and micromanaging, it's just, like, fits.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:39.000
Fallon Looney: That's so amazing. I think that's lovely, too, about the nostalgia side of it. I feel like in terms of these problem solution questions, I feel like I got pretty clear on their priorities and wants and needs. What would you say they're most concerned about when it comes to this aspect?

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:26.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:34.000
Lauren Christensen: Okay.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:58.000
Fallon Looney: and that could be, like, what they're most concerned about in terms of, like, what's the most important to them on their wedding day, but it could also, I think, lean into any, like, hesitations or things that might turn them off that you're able to, like, circumvent from how amazing, um, your space is, if that makes sense.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:48.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:58.000
Lauren Christensen: Sure.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:19.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, so I think in terms of like concerns and what they're worried about and how we solve that would be concerned that this is gonna be like another full-time job on top of their busy lives is planning this whole wedding. So, you know, we solved that with simplifying with these packages. So I think that's concerns. I think in terms of like desires and how we solve that, they really just want to have like a great party.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:31.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, so we're kind of a non-traditional space, you know, when compared to most of the other spots in our area, so we tend to attract clients that are a little bit less tradition, they're not as worried about, like, you know.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:53.000
Lauren Christensen: having a church wedding, or around here, you know, barns are so common. They like that our space is, like, a little edgier, a little bit different, because it kind of matches their personality. They're not so, like, in this box that they feel like they have to do things a certain way. And so, for them, like, having an epic party, you know, that's super fun and casual, but has kind of cool cocktails and a neat space, and they're right downtown.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:18:02.000
Lauren Christensen: And, you know, after the part… after the wedding's done, they can continue the party by, like, walking to one of the neighborhood bars within a block or two of us. Like, that's a priority for them, if that.

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:18.000
Fallon Looney: Yep, that makes total sense. And I think really leading with that overall experience, the party, their priorities. I think that's so great. I think we really nailed down this question with that answer. Is there anything specific in terms of like.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:33.000
Fallon Looney: reasons why the… the couple slash the bride, um, should book with you guys? Are there any specific unique benefits, aside from what we've already talked about, that… that you think would be relevant?

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:50.000
Lauren Christensen: Not anything really additionally. You know, I think it's the fact typically that we kind of check the box of like simplifying the planning. We check the box of having a cool, unique space. We're in slow. You know, another thing I guess that kind of resonates is that these people are coming from the Bay in LA where our type of venue is common.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:19:05.000
Lauren Christensen: So they've seen that, or, you know, they like to go to cocktail bars and have, like, a craft cocktail, and they want to have that same experience for their wedding, but they can do that in the place… A, in a place that, even if they didn't go to Cal Poly, that's… it's more affordable here than to get married in either.

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:17.000
Lauren Christensen: you know, go to the wineries and do wine tasting, have a beach experience. Like, they're not in the city, but they get that kind of city feel and, like, that cultural feel within our venue, if that makes sense.

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:32.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah, sounds so beautiful. All right. Are there any questions, like any FAQs that you tend to get from brides and couples that come up in your interactions or meetings before they book?

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:44.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, I mean, one of the main ones is, like, you know, how much is all this gonna cost? Obviously. Um, and I think that's one thing that's a struggle, which I think I put in my brochure, or in some of my answers, is…

00:19:44.000 --> 00:20:05.000
Lauren Christensen: because there's not a lot of all-inclusives where we are, we are always getting compared to, like, you know, because they're… all the other venues in our area that they're touring when they come tour us are venue rental only, so they're bringing all the stuff in, right? So, they might see a venue rental cost of $10,000, and then they're looking at our cost, which is $30, and they clearly know there's more in there, but they don't really know the value of those things.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:19.000
Lauren Christensen: So people are kind of wanting guidance, just like, what's all in? What's my wedding going to cost? So that's one. I think people do want to know when they're looking at our venue and comparing us, because we are urban and downtown.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:35.000
Lauren Christensen: And then they're looking at a venue that's 15 minutes from us, and it's on, you know, 15 acres. Like, why would I pick somewhere that's downtown? Because most of our clients are drawn to the urban thing, they love that, that's like, it checks their box immediately, but then we have some clients that are kind of torn between the two, right?

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:36.000
Lauren Christensen: Um…

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:51.000
Lauren Christensen: So I don't know, I think they're just trying to figure out, like, what does a wedding weekend look like downtown? And so if we can kind of map that out for them and showcase, you know, what a cool experience it is to be downtown, that's, I think, a question that we get sometimes too.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:21:03.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah, for sure. And I think also your point around, like, the whole experience, they can make a weekend out of it, that really, you know, I think plays a part for sure. Um…

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:12.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, you know, those… most of our other venues in the area, because they're not… they're mostly, like, you know, run under an Airbnb permit, so people are… there's accommodations on site, which is one thing we.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:08.000
Fallon Looney: Right.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.000
Fallon Looney: Thank you.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:32.000
Lauren Christensen: But, you know, it's a venue rental only, so they're realistically going to spend $15,000 to $20,000 more at one of our competitors, you know, quote-unquote competitors. But it's hard for them to know that at the beginning, because they just see the venue rental fee, they don't realize the cost of bringing in and outsourcing all those things. So beyond the cost, you know, way more time involved.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:37.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, but yeah, I would say mostly it's about, like, what does it look like down here?

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:56.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah, for sure. Are there any particular objections that come up, um, and any way that you address those objections? I'm sure it's typically always, like, price or budget or things like that, but is there anything else noteworthy that you think.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:53.000
Lauren Christensen: Okay.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:57.000
Fallon Looney: Um…

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:15.000
Lauren Christensen: I mean, sometimes it comes down to us… space is a concern for our clients, you know, because we can really only comfortably… the max we recommend for a wedding, where there's a seated ceremony, seated meals, like 125. Um, and so we do a lot of weddings, like, around that 100, 125, and so I think for people.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:21:59.000
Fallon Looney: Yes, ma'am.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:02.000
Fallon Looney: Mmhm.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:31.000
Lauren Christensen: that are kind of on the cusp there, like, we've got to kind of walk through what that looks like. And so how we address that is, you know, in the tour, you know, trying to map that out. Typically, we like to have furniture and things displayed in there so they can physically see what things look like, and if it's a wedding day and we're doing a tour, that.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:32.000
Lauren Christensen: Um…

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:50.000
Lauren Christensen: So, spacing, just people want to make sure it's not going to be, you know, too small for them. It's great for those, like, intimate, you know, smaller weddings. There's not a lot of area… most of the venues in our area are so big that, like, a 50-person wedding would just feel weird in, like, a giant barn. So, for those clients, we're a great fit, too. You.

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:56.000
Lauren Christensen: That's like a natural win, but it's sometimes that size is limiting for the larger weddings.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:07.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah, for sure. And I think if you are having a little bit of a smaller wedding, you don't want it to feel so big and overwhelming for the party size, too. So, appealing.

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:04.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:24.000
Lauren Christensen: And that's what I tell them, you know, we don't… you know, it's not gonna feel, like, vacuous in here, and empty, or it's not gonna feel like you break up the party, um, and especially once you get furniture and things in there, or you're not gonna have to, like, bring a bunch in to fill it, because typically when we're off-site, you know, catering, and it's a small wedding.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:08.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:11.000
Fallon Looney: Right.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:14.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah. Yep.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:17.000
Fallon Looney: Mm-hmm.

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:21.000
Fallon Looney: Mmhm.

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:33.000
Fallon Looney: Oh, yeah, for sure. For sure. I love it. If you could tell brides one thing about booking with you, what would it be?

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:43.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, we're gonna make this whole process fun, um, easy, and then you guys are gonna have a great party day of.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:45.000
Fallon Looney: I love it.

00:23:45.000 --> 00:24:03.000
Fallon Looney: We'll just go through, these remaining questions because I think that these are always, great. What would you say it makes your service transform the nice to have? I mean, I think that yours is really a necessity, but, from nice to have to a must have.

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:52.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:05.000
Fallon Looney: For… for brides and couples.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.000
Lauren Christensen: I think once they really…

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:24.000
Lauren Christensen: I think a lot of them come into this already feeling overwhelmed, right? So they kind of already know, like, that it's gonna be… it could be potentially a very overwhelming process. Some of them have that on their radar already. But I think once we get in and we're able to talk to them and kind of show the value of what we provide.

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:39.000
Lauren Christensen: how easy we're gonna make it for them, how we're gonna, like, support them and guide them through the whole planning process. It's not like they just book the venue, and then they gotta do everything else on their own. Once they see, and we can build that trust to see how we're gonna do that for them, then it's kinda like, okay, this makes sense.

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:56.000
Fallon Looney: Yep, yep. And I feel like we did touch on this a bit, but what would you say makes you stand out from competitors? Or if choosing between you and something similar, what do you think are factors that would really tip the scale for them in your favor?

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:53.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:06.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, I think just all that's bundled into the package, so, like, how much, really, it is a full-service experience for them, um…

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:15.000
Lauren Christensen: you know, how much we do, how much we handle, um, I think that's our competitor, because… or that's our competitive advantage, um, because there aren't…

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:31.000
Lauren Christensen: any… visually, there's not really anyone that compares to us. There's a couple… there's, like, one other downtown venue, but, um, that's kind of what sets us apart. And then, same thing, you know, we are compared to these places that physically don't look anything like us, just because of the nature of what the options are in the area. Um, but when they.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:37.000
Lauren Christensen: what all comes with our package versus what they have to do elsewhere. That, hopefully, is the advantage there.

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:40.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah, I love it.

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:54.000
Fallon Looney: Any questions on your end, Whitney, that might have come up? I feel like we flew through that form, but you did such a great job with your questionnaire. I also didn't want to be too repetitive. Um, anything else coming to mind, Whitney, on your end?

00:25:52.000 --> 00:26:08.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, just a couple of things that I am curious about that kind of came up over the course of the conversation. This first one is more even just like my curiosity, right? I'm not sure how much it needs to be woven in to ad strategy at all.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:19.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, but, um, is there a history or a story behind the name The Penny? Did, is, is that your daughter's name? Did I hear you? Okay. Yeah. Mm-hmm.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:35.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, mm-hmm. Yeah, so… we make it sound much better than it was, but essentially, when we were trying to figure out… so the building used to be, like, a blacksmithing shop. It was built in the 30s as a blacksmithing shop, so with that history, we were trying to come up with a name that had to do with, like, the metalworking, or some way to tie the history of the building in, um…

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:34.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:42.000
Lauren Christensen: We, at the time, had two other partners, and, like, none of us could just really agree on a name, or we couldn't really find anything that we liked.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:55.000
Lauren Christensen: But… so we kept kind of throwing some stuff out there, and nothing was really sticking, and then our partners actually were like, what about penny? You know, you have your daughter's penny, there's copper, you know, there's a saying, like, it's pretty as a penny. Um…

00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:05.000
Lauren Christensen: They had had a wishing well at their wedding, where people tossed pennies in and wrote little, like, advice notes for their wedding, and so between the two of us, we had some connection there, and so we kind of just ran with that.

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:02.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:07.000
Whitney Hodges: Very cute.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:21.000
Lauren Christensen: But also, just kind of a fun story, the trippy thing was, too, we found out, like, 4 months into the construction process, and we have a really cool little mini, like, history museum about our city and our county that's a couple blocks from us. So we went over there, and we were able to pull, like, some records of what.

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Bye.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:36.000
Lauren Christensen: the downtown wasn't a downtown, you know, so when our… the people that built the building built it, it was their farmland, and they had their shop there, and they had… there was a barn across the street where there's a shopping center now, and um… so they lived in… the home was right next to our building, so we found pictures of the home, and there was.

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:49.000
Lauren Christensen: there was a wishing well actually on… in their front yard, which we didn't know about. We had already landed on the name, so it was kind of a cool little, like, tie together. So I usually incorporate that kind of in my story if I can, and I have pictures of the building and stuff if you guys ever needed.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:28:10.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, awesome. Yeah, that's, that's super cute. Okay. Um, I think what else came up for me, I think what was really, um, an important thing that you mentioned, um, was just the, the, the Cal Poly thread in there and that couples may be coming from, um, other cities. We've definitely seen that with other venues where.

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:07.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:26.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, they want to go back to, kind of, like, their college town, or where they met, and that's really cool, because we can actually even, like, layer into… play around with layering into the interests, like Cal Poly as an interest, too, and we've found success with that, so that's cool.

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:31.000
Whitney Hodges: I think what I'm curious about, because it does sound like your venue, um…

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:48.000
Whitney Hodges: Is, is pretty unique, right, for, for San Luis Obispo. I'm curious though, when couples come to you, have they already kind of decided, yes, we definitely want to get married in San Luis Obispo, or is it ever like, oh, we're deciding between.

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:58.000
Whitney Hodges: doing it there, or maybe doing it in LA, or doing it in the Bay Area, like, where we actually live. Um, just kind of wanted some more insight on, like.

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:55.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:04.000
Whitney Hodges: If that is a part of the decision, um, when couples are typically coming to you.

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:01.000
Lauren Christensen: Hmm.

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:20.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, I mean, I would say sometimes that is a conversation that I have, they're undecided, um, or sometimes if they… not always, I mean, the majority are, and I try to kind of gauge that, you know, when they come in for the tour, but most of them are like, yeah, we're definitely gonna get married in this area, we're just not sure if you're the spot or not. But I do.

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:10.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:27.000
Lauren Christensen: Maybe 10 to 20% of our clients are still considering closer to home kind of situation.

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:46.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, good to know. And with it primarily, I mean, really kind of being destination weddings and you not having accommodations on site, what do you typically see that they're doing for their guests? If any, like, are they doing like room blocks at nearby hotels or people just kind of like Airbnb and do their own thing?

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:34.000
Lauren Christensen: Okay.

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:37.000
Lauren Christensen: Mmhm.

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:49.000
Lauren Christensen: Yes, excuse me. So within…

00:29:49.000 --> 00:30:05.000
Lauren Christensen: 2 blocks of us. There's 2, 3 hotel options. So one of them you can actually see it from our patios and I'm giving the tour. I can be like, oh, this is one right here that so we basically had 2 in 2022 new boutique hotels opened right downtown within 2 blocks of us.

00:29:58.000 --> 00:29:59.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:29.000
Lauren Christensen: So typically we see between those two, like the majority of couples and maybe bridal party family, people more definitely involved in the wedding are staying right downtown. And we kind of push that because they can not have to get in the car the whole weekend. They just, you know, make their base downtown. And we also have another cool option a block and a half from us that is more of an Airbnb style and it's on Higuera Street, which is our main like downtown street with all the shops and restaurants.

00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:17.000
Whitney Hodges: I'll push that because.

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:27.000
Whitney Hodges: Thank you.

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:49.000
Lauren Christensen: Second story, there's, like, five lofts there that they've done. You can rent them individually, like a hotel room, but if you book all five, it's like an Airbnb right in the downtown core, so we send a lot of business over there, too. So, you know, usually I would say, yeah, wedding parties staying down here, those hotels are pretty expensive, so it's typically a mix of, like, people more involved right downtown.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:32.000
Whitney Hodges: All right, bye.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:36.000
Whitney Hodges: Hotel.

00:30:46.000 --> 00:30:48.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm, yes.

00:30:49.000 --> 00:31:02.000
Lauren Christensen: And then about a mile and a half from us, up by the university, there's kind of like a hotel strip with probably 15 additional hotels. So it's usually a mix of, like, some right downtown and then some staying out there, um, typically.

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:15.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, awesome. Good. Yeah, I think that helps, you know, just paint more of a picture, right? Um, and potentially, Fallon, if you think it's useful, like, that could maybe even be woven into some of the FAQs on the ads traffic landing page.

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:08.000
Lauren Christensen: Mmhm.

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:15.000
Fallon Looney: Mm-hmm.

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:17.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm. Yeah.

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:32.000
Fallon Looney: Yep, yeah, exactly, because that I'm sure that's something I mean, at least in the planning process is going to be coming up for them in terms of what that's going to look like, and knowing that there's, you know, accommodations in the surrounding area. It's something they have to be prepared for for their guests, especially if it's.

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:23.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:29.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:40.000
Fallon Looney: where they went to college, and they're, you know, gonna have a bit of a drive. So yeah, I think that's great. That was a good point.

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:37.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:56.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, and I think, you know, on our current website, we've got a whole, like, lodging page that, um, lists out, you know, the 15 nearest hotels with mileage and all that kind of on there. Um, so that's typically something we're kind of talking about in the tour, and then following up with, like, our sales collateral to kind of, like, link that back for them, too.

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:51.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay. Sorry.

00:31:57.000 --> 00:31:58.000
Whitney Hodges: Perfect.

00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:18.000
Lauren Christensen: We also have on our current website, which I think a lot of it was from Snomed, was like a combo of like an SEO thing, but also trying to paint that weekend, like what that would look like. There's a whole like wedding weekend itinerary and it kind of maps some of that stuff out. So I'll be talking with the Celebrate Creative people if that's something we think we want to incorporate into the website, you know, continually, which I think it's been valuable for people.

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:34.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, perfect. Yeah, just kind of another potential thought, um, on that, like, if you like the direction of this, maybe share it with Amy, or if you like the direction, it could be something that we pull into the ads traffic landing page. For a venue like yours, right, when people are, um.

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:27.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:50.000
Whitney Hodges: comparing, but it's not an apples-to-apples sort of comparison, right, when you're all-inclusive and everybody else is, like, venue-only. Um, sometimes what we have seen worked well is almost like… almost like a comparison chart sort of thing, right? Where it's like, the penny, this is what's included… you have one already?

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:38.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah.

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:42.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm.

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:49.000
Lauren Christensen: Mm-hmm. Yeah, we have one of those.

00:32:49.000 --> 00:33:10.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, we have it in our current website, like a venue comparison chart. I do think, like, visually it could look better than it does, it's just kind of basic looking, um, but I have… I do think that that's helpful, and I try to kind of emphasize that with people in follow-up. When I'm doing my follow-ups after tours, or even if they've got a lot of questions ahead of time, yeah, like, look at everything we have, and then look at, like, what you're gonna.

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:52.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:15.000
Lauren Christensen: do on your own and, like, here's an estimated cost for what that will cost you to do that outside of this.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:20.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, perfect. I must have missed that on the website. Do you know off the top of your head which of the pages it's on?

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:21.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, let me see here.

00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:36.000
Whitney Hodges: So I do think on the landing page that that would be pretty powerful to include.

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:38.000
Fallon Looney: A hundred percent.

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:43.000
Lauren Christensen: So, if you… if you see the FAQ tab, and you hover on that, and then there's a little thing underneath that says Venue Comparison…

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:45.000
Whitney Hodges: Oh, okay.

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:52.000
Lauren Christensen: And we… it used to be a little bit more detailed. She… what Elizabeth recommended, like, simplifying it down, because it had, like, a lot of words on it, but I do.

00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:53.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:57.000
Lauren Christensen: almost too simple now, or I'm trying to figure out a way to, like, communicate more value than…

00:33:56.000 --> 00:33:58.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:58.000
Lauren Christensen: What's just there?

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, I could see this like visually almost just like being a little prettier, right? And instead of included like cute like check marks and things like that. But this is great to like have the information and then we can kind of like go from there in terms of how to design it.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:00.000
Lauren Christensen: And that kind of breaks.

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:05.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, exactly.

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:07.000
Lauren Christensen: Uh-huh.

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:12.000
Lauren Christensen: Then…

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:16.000
Fallon Looney: Right. Mm-hmm. For sure.

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:15.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:20.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, but yeah, I had totally mentioned that, because I, uh, missed that, because I feel like I clicked on…

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:26.000
Whitney Hodges: just FAQs, and it took me to a different page that has all of the FAQs versus a drop-down thing.

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:27.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah, it's probably not as prominent as it could be.

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:44.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah. Thank you for that. That's awesome. But, yeah, I mean, I feel like we have so much good stuff to work with just based on what you've already shared, what already exists on the website. So we're excited to get started on all of it. Looking forward to, I won't be on the call tomorrow for the the lead lock onboarding.

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:57.000
Whitney Hodges: But Fallon will be there, and you'll be in great hands with Danielle, and then we'll regroup again next week, um, to just talk about the ad strategy in more detail, and then really just kind of deploy from there.

00:34:57.000 --> 00:34:58.000
Lauren Christensen: Okay, awesome.

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:01.000
Whitney Hodges: Any questions for us in the meantime?

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:14.000
Lauren Christensen: I don't think so at this point. Um, oh, I guess my one question was, so I know you kind of talked about, like, a three-layered approach, right, for the different types of reach that we're trying to do, so…

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:11.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Right.

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:23.000
Lauren Christensen: Do each of those have separate creative, too, in terms of, like, what we're putting out there for people, or is it all one… one creative, and then you're just targeting different ways?

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:43.000
Whitney Hodges: No, it's really different objectives for each layer of the funnel, because, um, you know, there are different things that we want to accomplish with those, and people are going to be kind of at different stages as well, right? So, for completely, like, cold audiences, client attraction is what we call it.

00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:33.000
Lauren Christensen: Sure.

00:35:43.000 --> 00:36:02.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, usually what we will do, um, and what Fallon will most likely have prepared before our next call is identifying some of your existing Instagram Reels that are strong that could be, like, repurposed as those creatives. And so for those, then, it doesn't feel so much like an ad or, like, salesy or anything like that. It's more just, like, people.

00:36:02.000 --> 00:36:18.000
Whitney Hodges: Coming across you in the newsfeed or in their stories, right? Um, it's kind of an introduction to the brand, um, versus the conversion campaigns where the goal really is to get them to inquire, to get them to book a tour. That is going to feel more like an ad, right? Because it's direct response. We're asking something.

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:34.000
Whitney Hodges: of them. Um, and then bottom of funnel is typically retargeting ads, so warm audience members, people who maybe have already inquired or even scheduled, um, a tour. Um, for those, again, we like for it to feel a bit more organic.

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:57.000
Whitney Hodges: But the purpose is very different than just introducing the penny to them. It's more like the why of why they should book with you, overcoming some of those objectives, like talking about why it would be better to go with you than a competitor, how all-inclusive is going to be so much more stress-free than the alternative sort of thing. So it's more meant to be.

00:36:57.000 --> 00:37:01.000
Whitney Hodges: Content that you would want to share that goes deeper than just.

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:06.000
Whitney Hodges: Hey, look at our beautiful venue that you've never heard of before, if that makes sense.

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:08.000
Lauren Christensen: Yeah. Great.

00:37:08.000 --> 00:37:31.000
Lauren Christensen: Um, and I don't know if you guys have had a chance to look at that, or if there's… I basically made a Google Drive folder for you with, like, all those full… all the images, um, so I have way more than that, obviously, so if you feel like you need more in there, or if you need more context, because I feel like all the images I took were obviously ones I think are strong, but they were really from, like, our ideal clients, too, right? So, aesthetically, like, what was attractive to them, um.

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:17.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:27.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:30.000
Whitney Hodges: Sure.

00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:48.000
Lauren Christensen: because we've got kind of a couple different looks, you know, obviously all weddings look different, but there's interesting, like, certain ideal clients seem to be attracted visually to, like, certain types of things. I try to kind of put all those in there, but um… I guess just letting you know, if you have questions or you want more context or need more images, just let me know and I can pull them.

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:35.000
Whitney Hodges: Lucky you, I think.

00:37:48.000 --> 00:38:03.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, perfect. That's good to know. But yeah, the fact that you've already kind of culled some of the best images and of what appeals to your ideal clients, I would guess that we're probably going to have enough to work with from there. Um, but yeah, we'll definitely reach out if there's ever.

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:16.000
Lauren Christensen: Okay.

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:19.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, perfect.

